To address the delays and improve the performance of your integrations, you can leverage asynchronous programming techniques. By making the calls to your services asynchronous, you can significantly reduce the overall latency. Here's how you can refactor your code to use Java's `CompletableFuture` for asynchronous execution:

### Refactored Code Using `CompletableFuture`

```java
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class Integration {
    public void integrate() {
        // Start all services asynchronously
        CompletableFuture<Void> service1Future = CompletableFuture.runAsync(this::callService1);
        CompletableFuture<Void> service2Future = CompletableFuture.runAsync(this::callService2);
        CompletableFuture<Void> service3Future = CompletableFuture.runAsync(this::callService3);

        // Wait for all services to complete
        CompletableFuture.allOf(service1Future, service2Future, service3Future).join();
    }

    private void callService1() {
        Service1 service1 = new Service1();
        service1.call();
    }

    private void callService2() {
        Service2 service2 = new Service2();
        service2.call();
    }

    private void callService3() {
        Service3 service3 = new Service3();
        service3.call();
    }
}

public class Service1 {
    public void call() {
        // Simulate delay
        try {
            Thread.sleep(200);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}

public class Service2 {
